## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, "change" means a different value. For objects and functions, it means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// This creates a NEW object on every render of Parent
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// This creates a NEW function on every render of Parent
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every time `Parent` renders, `Child` gets new prop references, so `Child` re-renders too — even if nothing meaningful changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
- Use when: a child component is expensive to render and its props don't change often.
- Pointless without also stabilizing the props you pass it.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
- Use when: you pass a callback to a `memo`-wrapped child, or it's a dependency in another hook.
- Not worth it for functions never passed down or used as deps.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value.
- Use when: a calculation is genuinely expensive, or you need a stable object/array reference to pass as a prop.
- Don't reach for it by default — the cache itself has overhead.

The three work together: `memo` on the child is useless if you keep passing it fresh objects/functions. You need all three in concert.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** when the context value changes. The common mistake is creating the value inline:

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every render of App
function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>
      <Page />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Every time `App` re-renders for any reason, all `ThemeContext` consumers re-render.

Fixes:
- `useMemo` on the context value object.
- Split contexts — separate rarely-changing state from frequently-changing state.
- Lift the provider high enough that it rarely re-renders itself.

---

### 4. Before/After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` renders, even when `items` hasn't changed.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = (id) => {           // new ref every render
    console.log('selected', id);
  };

  const options = { sortBy: 'name' };      // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicks: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={handleSelect}
        options={options}
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, options }) {
  // renders on every Parent render
  return <ul>{items.map(i => <li key={i.id} onClick={() => onSelect(i.id)}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {   // stable reference
    console.log('selected', id);
  }, []);                                       // no deps → never changes

  const options = useMemo(() => ({              // stable reference
    sortBy: 'name'
  }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicks: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={handleSelect}
        options={options}
      />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect, options }) {
  // only re-renders when items/onSelect/options references change
  return <ul>{items.map(i => <li key={i.id} onClick={() => onSelect(i.id)}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
});
```

---

**Key rule of thumb:** measure before optimizing. Use React DevTools Profiler to confirm a component is actually re-rendering unnecessarily — premature memoization adds complexity and its own overhead without guaranteed benefit.
